    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN115-193-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF After subtracting her expenses from her take-home income, Ash calculated (assuming that there are exactly 52 weeks in a year and 26 fortnights in a year) that at the end of last year she was left with a surplus of $18 552 to use for a holiday or deposit in a savings account. (c) Ash hopes that her budget updates for the current year will lead to an increase in her annual surplus. Determine, with justification, whether this is the case. (4 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN115-193-4 After subtracting her expenses from her take-home income, Ash calculated (assuming that there are exactly 52 weeks in a year and 26 fortnights in a year) that at the end of last year she was left with a surplus of $18 552 to use for a holiday or deposit in a savings account. (c) Ash hopes that her budget updates for the current year will lead to an increase in her annual surplus. Determine, with justification, whether this is the case. (4 marks) Solution Income: 𝐼𝐼= 2173.50 × 26 = $56 511 Weekly costs: 𝐶𝐶𝑊𝑊= (25.50 + 170.00 + 135.00 + 157.50) × 52 = 488 × 52 = $25 376 Monthly costs: 𝐶𝐶𝑀𝑀= (45.25 + 506.75 + 427.50) × 12 = 979.50 × 12 = $11 754 Annual surplus: 𝑆𝑆= 56 511 −(25 376 + 11 754) = 56 511 −37 130 = $19 381 Hence her annual surplus has slightly increased from $18 552 to $19 381 - which is as she had hoped. Specific behaviours  correctly adjusts fortnightly income to annual  correctly adjusts weekly costs to annual  correctly adjusts monthly costs to annual  calculates surplus and notes it has increased",
