    "calculator": "Calculator-free",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN245-133-1 Question 3 (9 marks) Four matrices are defined as 𝐴= [ 4 −1 6 ] , 𝐵= [2 1 −1 7 −2 3 ] , 𝐶= [ 1 3 −2 0] and 𝐷= [5 −4]. (a) State (i) which matrix has more rows than columns. (1 mark) (ii) the dimensions of the row matrix. (1 mark) (b) Calculate 5𝐶+ 3𝐼, where 𝐼 is the 2 × 2 identity matrix. (3 marks) (c) Calculate 𝐶2. (2 marks) (d) It is required to multiply all four matrices together so that the final product is a square matrix with the largest possible number of columns. State the order in which the matrices should be multiplied and state the number of columns in the final product. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN245-133-2 Question 3 (9 marks) Four matrices are defined as 𝐴= [ 4 −1 6 ] , 𝐵= [2 1 −1 7 −2 3 ] , 𝐶= [ 1 3 −2 0] and 𝐷= [5 −4]. (a) State (i) which matrix has more rows than columns. (1 mark) (ii) the dimensions of the row matrix. (1 mark) (b) Calculate 5𝐶+ 3𝐼, where 𝐼 is the 2 × 2 identity matrix. (3 marks) (c) Calculate 𝐶2. (2 marks) (d) It is required to multiply all four matrices together so that the final product is a square matrix with the largest possible number of columns. State the order in which the matrices should be multiplied and state the number of columns in the final product. (2 marks) Solution Matrix 𝐴 Specific behaviours ✓ correct name Solution 5 × [ 1 3 −2 0] = [ 5 15 −10 0 ] 3𝐼= [3 0 0 3] 5𝐶+ 3𝐼= [ 5 15 −10 0 ] + [3 0 0 3] = [ 8 15 −10 3 ] Specific behaviours ✓ correct multiple of 𝐶 ✓ correct multiple of 𝐼 ✓ correct sum Solution [ 1 3 −2 0] × [ 1 3 −2 0] = [−5 3 −2 −6] Specific behaviours ✓ one correct row or column ✓ correct product Solution Size of matrix 𝐷 is 1 × 2 Specific behaviours ✓ correct size Solution Order: 𝐴× 𝐷× 𝐶× 𝐵. Product will have 3 columns. Specific behaviours ✓ correct order ✓ correct numbe",
