    "calculator": "Calculator-free",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-133-5 Question 3 (9 marks) Four matrices are defined as 𝐴= [ 4 −9] , 𝐵= [ 3 4 6 −7 −2 1 ] , 𝐶= [2 0 3 −1] and 𝐷= [1 0 5]. (a) State (i) which matrix has more columns than rows. (1 mark) (ii) the size of the column matrix. (1 mark) (b) Calculate 3𝐶−4𝐼, where 𝐼 is the 2 × 2 identity matrix. (3 marks) (c) Calculate 𝐶2. (2 marks) (d) It is possible to multiply all four matrices together so that the final product is a square matrix with more than two rows. State the order in which the matrices should be multiplied and state the number of columns in the final product. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-133-7 Question 3 (9 marks) Four matrices are defined as 𝐴= [ 4 −9] , 𝐵= [ 3 4 6 −7 −2 1 ] , 𝐶= [2 0 3 −1] and 𝐷= [1 0 5]. (a) State (i) which matrix has more columns than rows. (1 mark) (ii) the size of the column matrix. (1 mark) (b) Calculate 3𝐶−4𝐼, where 𝐼 is the 2 × 2 identity matrix. (3 marks) (c) Calculate 𝐶2. (2 marks) (d) It is possible to multiply all four matrices together so that the final product is a square matrix with more than two rows. State the order in which the matrices should be multiplied and state the number of columns in the final product. (2 marks) Solution Matrix 𝐷 Specific behaviours ✓ correct name Solution 3 × [2 0 3 −1] = [6 0 9 −3] 4𝐼= [4 0 0 4] 3𝐶−4𝐼= [6 0 9 −3] −[4 0 0 4] = [2 0 9 −7] Specific behaviours ✓ correct multiple of 𝐶 ✓ correct multiple of 𝐼 ✓ correct difference Solution [2 0 3 −1] × [2 0 3 −1] = [4 0 3 1] Specific behaviours ✓ one correct row or column ✓ correct product Solution Size of matrix 𝐴 is 2 × 1 Specific behaviours ✓ correct size Solution Order: 𝐵× 𝐶× 𝐴× 𝐷. Product will have 3 columns. Specific behaviours ✓ correct order ✓ correct number of columns",
