    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-153-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 12 (12 marks) A fishing store make their own conical and spherical lead weights for fishermen. To do this, they buy a block of lead costing $335 from their supplier in the form of rectangular prism that measures 65 × 65 × 124 mm. The store then melt the lead and use casts to produce their own weights. The conical weights have a radius of 7 mm and a perpendicular height of 16 mm, and the spherical weights have a diameter of 12 mm. The store uses the block of lead to make a batch of 250 conical and 350 spherical weights which are then sold for $1.55 and $1.75 respectively. (a) Determine the volume of lead required by the store to make all the conical weights. (3 marks) (b) Determine the volume of lead required to make all the spherical weights. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-153-8 Question 12 (12 marks) A fishing store make their own conical and spherical lead weights for fishermen. To do this, they buy a block of lead costing $335 from their supplier in the form of rectangular prism that measures 65 × 65 × 124 mm. The store then melt the lead and use casts to produce their own weights. The conical weights have a radius of 7 mm and a perpendicular height of 16 mm, and the spherical weights have a diameter of 12 mm. The store uses the block of lead to make a batch of 250 conical and 350 spherical weights which are then sold for $1.55 and $1.75 respectively. (a) Determine the volume of lead required by the store to make all the conical weights. (3 marks) (b) Determine the volume of lead required to make all the spherical weights. (3 marks) Solution 𝑟= 12 ÷ 2 = 6 𝑉𝑆= 4 3 × 𝜋× 63 = 904.78 350 × 𝑉𝑆= 316 673 mm3 Specific behaviours  uses correct radius and substitutes  volume of one sphere  total volume of spheres See note on rounding in (c) Solution 𝑉𝐶= 𝜋× 72 × 16 3 = 821.0 250 × 𝑉𝐶= 205 251 mm3 Specific behaviours  correctly substitutes  volume of one cone  total volume of cones See note on",
