    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 16 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED End of questions SN085-153-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 21 (7 marks) A supermarket sells identical lollies in packs of 55, 28 and 15 for $9.20, $4.20 and $2.70 respectively. (a) Use the unit cost method to rank the pack sizes in order from best to worst value. (3 marks) (b) The supermarket has suggested to the manufacturer that they should produce a pack containing 40 lollies. (i) Determine the minimum price of this pack so that it is worse value than all the existing sizes, giving your answer to the nearest 5c. (2 marks) (ii) Determine the maximum price of this pack so that it is better value than all the existing sizes, giving your answer to the nearest 5c. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 16 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED End of questions SN085-153-8 Question 21 (7 marks) A supermarket sells identical lollies in packs of 55, 28 and 15 for $9.20, $4.20 and $2.70 respectively. (a) Use the unit cost method to rank the pack sizes in order from best to worst value. (3 marks) (b) The supermarket has suggested to the manufacturer that they should produce a pack containing 40 lollies. (i) Determine the minimum price of this pack so that it is worse value than all the existing sizes, giving your answer to the nearest 5c. (2 marks) (ii) Determine the maximum price of this pack so that it is better value than all the existing sizes, giving your answer to the nearest 5c. (2 marks) Solution Each lolly must cost 15c: Pack price: 40 × 0.15 = $6.00, but this will be equal best value, so must charge $5.95 for it to be better value. Specific behaviours  price for equal best value  correct price Solution Each lolly must cost 18c: Pack price: 40 × 0.18 = $7.20, but this will be equal worst value, so must charge $7.25 for it to be worse value. Specific behaviours  price for equal worst value  correct price Solution 55-pack: 9.20 ÷ 55 = 0.167 ea 28-pack: 4.20 ÷ 28 = 0.15 ea 1",
