    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-153-1 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 3 (7 marks) When investigating the costs associated with running a 125cc motorbike, a student estimated the following figures for their budget. Frequency Expense Monthly ($) Half-yearly ($) Servicing 90 Registration 60 Insurance 48 Loan repayment 124 Fuel 85 Wax and polish 44 (a) Briefly explain what the term 'fixed spending' means in relation to a budget and give an example that could apply to the running costs of a motorbike. (2 marks) (b) Briefly explain what the term 'discretionary spending' means in relation to a budget and give an example that could apply to the running costs of a motorbike. (2 marks) (c) Use the figures in the table to determine if a monthly budget of $350 is enough to cover the running costs of the motorbike. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 5 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-153-7 Question 3 (7 marks) When investigating the costs associated with running a 125cc motorbike, a student estimated the following figures for their budget. Frequency Expense Monthly ($) Half-yearly ($) Servicing 90 Registration 60 Insurance 48 Loan repayment 124 Fuel 85 Wax and polish 44 (a) Briefly explain what the term 'fixed spending' means in relation to a budget and give an example that could apply to the running costs of a motorbike. (2 marks) (b) Briefly explain what the term 'discretionary spending' means in relation to a budget and give an example that could apply to the running costs of a motorbike. (2 marks) (c) Use the figures in the table to determine if a monthly budget of $350 is enough to cover the running costs of the motorbike. (3 marks) Solution Discretionary spending is spending on non-essential items or services (spending on \"wants\" rather than \"needs\"). Examples: Spending on wax and polish, buying accessories, etc. Specific behaviours  reasonable explanation  satisfactory example Solution 90 ÷ 6 = $15, 60 ÷ 6 = $10, 48 ÷ 6 = $8. 15 + 10 + 8 + 124 + 85 + 44 = $286/month. Hence budget of $350 is more",
