    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 14 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-193-8 Question 18 (9 marks) Covid created a travel bubble in 2021 where many people from both Australia and New Zealand would travel only across the Tasman. Mary changed some Australian dollars ($AUD) into New Zealand dollars($NZD). The exchange rate was given as $1AUD = $1.07NZD. She exchanged $2000 AUD. (a) How many $NZD did she receive? (1 marks) Solution 2000 x 1.07 = $NZD2140 Specific behaviours  correct $NZD currency given (b) After spending most of her $NZD, she had $300NZD left. What was the value of this in $AUD? (2 marks) Solution Specific behaviours  Divides by 1.07  Correct answer r/w (c) Determine the conversion rate of $NZD to $1AUD. (2 marks) Solution Rate = Specific behaviours  divides 1 by 1.07 Correct answer r/w Mary’s child had three notes; a $50, a $20 and a $10, but she didn’t know which were NZ and which were Australian (2 marks) (d) Using all three notes, what was the maximum value possible in Australian dollars, if one of the notes was from NZ? (2 marks) Solution Max value = Specific behaviours  shows 10 divided by 1.07 (ZN note) Correct max value",
