    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 7 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-193-1 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF (d) Determine the sum of the first 27 numbers in the sequence that starts 7, 14, 21, 28, …. (2 marks) Consider another formula 𝑇= 𝑛2𝑑 2 −𝑛𝑑 2 + 7𝑛. A claim was made that 𝑆 and 𝑇 will always be equal for any given value of 𝑑 and value of 𝑛. (e) Show that when 𝑛= 30 and 𝑑= 6 the claim is true. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 7 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-193-3 (d) Determine the sum of the first 27 numbers in the sequence that starts 7, 14, 21, 28, …. (2 marks) Consider another formula 𝑇= 𝑛2𝑑 2 −𝑛𝑑 2 + 7𝑛. A claim was made that 𝑆 and 𝑇 will always be equal for any given value of 𝑑 and value of 𝑛. (e) Show that when 𝑛= 30 and 𝑑= 6 the claim is true. (2 marks) Solution 𝑛= 27, 𝑑= 7 Hence 𝑆= 2646 from spreadsheet. Specific behaviours  indicates correct values of 𝑛 and 𝑑  correct value of 𝑆 Solution 𝑇= 302 × 6 2 −30 × 6 2 + 7 × 30 = 900 × 3 −90 + 210 = 2820 This is the same as the value of 𝑆 shown in the spreadsheet. Specific behaviours  substitutes and shows one correct simplification  shows further simplification before reaching result and noting same as 𝑆",
