    "calculator": "Calculator-free",
    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN085-233-1 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 3 (10 marks) Use these four matrices to answer the following questions. 𝐀= [5 −2] 𝐁= [2 6 5 −4] 𝐂= [4 6 5 −2] 𝐃= [ 4 −2] (a) Two of the following matrix calculations are not possible. 𝐀𝐁 𝐁𝐂 𝐁𝐀 𝐁+ 𝐂 𝐂+ 𝐃 Circle the two that are not possible and explain why. (4 marks) (b) Determine 𝐂−2𝐁. (2 marks) (c) Determine 𝐂𝐁. (2 marks) (d) Determine a matrix 𝐏 which, when added to 𝐁, results in a zero matrix. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN085-233-3 Question 3 (10 marks) Use these four matrices to answer the following questions. 𝐀= [5 −2] 𝐁= [2 6 5 −4] 𝐂= [4 6 5 −2] 𝐃= [ 4 −2] (a) Two of the following matrix calculations are not possible. 𝐀𝐁 𝐁𝐂 𝐁𝐀 𝐁+ 𝐂 𝐂+ 𝐃 Circle the two that are not possible and explain why. (4 marks) (b) Determine 𝐂−2𝐁. (2 marks) (c) Determine 𝐂𝐁. (2 marks) (d) Determine a matrix 𝐏 which, when added to 𝐁, results in a zero matrix. (2 marks) Solution 𝐏+ [2 6 5 −4] = [0 0 0 0] 𝐏= [−2 −6 −5 4 ] Specific behaviours ✓ Writes down a suitable equation or recognises that 𝐏 is the opposite of 𝐁. ✓ Determines 𝐏. Solution 𝐂𝐁= [4 6 5 −2] [2 6 5 −4] = [38 0 0 38] Specific behaviours ✓ any 2 × 2 matrix ✓ correct product Solution [4 6 5 −2] −2 [2 6 5 −4] = [4 6 5 −2] −[ 4 12 10 −8] = [ 0 −6 −5 6 ] Specific behaviours ✓ correct scalar multiple ✓ simplifies correctly to a single matrix Solution BA is not possible as the number of rows in B is not the same as the number of rows in A. C+D is not possible as they do not have the same dimensions. Specific behaviours ✓ Circles BA. ✓ Correct explanation of why BA cannot be determined. Accept: 2 × 2 1 ⏟ × 2 𝑛𝑜𝑡 𝑡ℎ𝑒 𝑠𝑎",
