    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 7 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-233-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF (d) What volume of gelato was left over? (1 mark) Giovanna used the leftover gelato to make two types of small frozen shapes - cubes with a side length of 14 mm and spheres with a radius of 9 mm. (e) Which shape used the least amount of gelato, and how much less gelato than the other shape? (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 7 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN085-233-4 (d) What volume of gelato was left over? (1 mark) Giovanna used the leftover gelato to make two types of small frozen shapes - cubes with a side length of 14 mm and spheres with a radius of 9 mm. (e) Which shape used the least amount of gelato, and how much less gelato than the other shape? (3 marks) Solution 𝑉𝐶= 143 = 2744 mm3 𝑉𝑆= 4 3 𝜋(9)3 = 3054 mm3 Δ = 3054 −2744 = 310 mm3 The cube used the least amount of gelato, using 310 mm3 less than the sphere. Specific behaviours ✓ volume of cube ✓ volume of sphere ✓ correctly states shape using least, and how much less, with units Solution 𝑉= 2650 −36 × 72 = 2650 −2592 = 58 cm3 58 cm3 of gelato was left over. Specific behaviours ✓ correct volume Alternate Solution 𝑉𝐶= 1.43 = 2.74 cm3 𝑉𝑆= 4 3 𝜋(0.9)3 = 3.05 cm3 Δ = 3.05 −2.74 = 0.31 cm3 The cube used the least amount of gelato, using 0.31 cm3 less than the sphere. Specific behaviours ✓ volume of cube ✓ volume of sphere ✓ correctly states shape using least, and how much less, with units",
