    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-253-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 10 (10 marks) The diagram below shows a prism of height 25 centimetres and detail of its cross section. The prisms are made of concrete and are used for garden edging. The cross section is a sector of a circle of radius 17.2 centimetres with a central angle of 220°. (a) Show that the area of the cross section is 568 square centimetres. Round the answer to the nearest square centimetres. (2 marks) (b) Determine the volume of the prism. Round the answer to the nearest cubic centimetre. (1 mark) (c) Determine the length of the circular arc of the cross section. Round the answer to the nearest centimetres. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 6 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN085-253-4 Question 10 (Rounding Question) (10 marks) The diagram below shows a prism of height 25 centimetres and detail of its cross section. The prisms are made of concrete and are used for garden edging. The cross section is a sector of a circle of radius 17.2 centimetres with a central angle of 220°. (a) Show that the area of the cross section is 568 square centimetres, round the answer to the nearest square centimetres. (2 marks) (b) Determine the volume of the prism. Round the answer to the nearest cubic centimetre. (1 mark) (c) Determine the length of the circular arc of the cross section, round the answer to the nearest centimetre. (2 marks) Solution 𝐿= 2 × 𝜋× 17.2 × 220 360 = 66.0 cm Specific behaviours ✓ correct expression for length ✓ correct length Solution 𝑉= 568 × 25 = 14 200 cm3 Specific behaviours ✓ correct volume of prism (F/T) Solution 𝐴= 𝜋× 17.22 × 220 360 = 567.97 ≈568 cm2 Specific behaviours ✓ shows correct calculation for area of whole circle ✓ shows multiplication by correct fraction",
