    "topicLabel": "Topic 1.1 · Consumer arithmetic",
    "calculator": "Calculator-free",
    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page Question 5 (6 marks) Hockey teams A and B had each played 21 games in a league. Team A had won 11, lost 4 and drawn the remainder of its games. In total, the two teams had won 21 and drawn 14 games. (a) Show this information in a 2 × 3 matrix 𝐻, with columns ordered win, lose, draw and rows ordered team A and team B. (2 marks) (b) Each team gains 3 points for a win, 1 point for a draw and no points for a loss. Show this information in matrix 𝑃 that can be multiplied by matrix 𝐻. (1 mark) (c) Calculate 𝐻× 𝑃 and state which team is leading the other in the league. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page (c) Calculate 𝐶2. (2 marks) Solution [1 3 −2 0 ] × [1 3 −2 0 ] = [−5 3 −2 −6 ] Specific behaviours ✔ one correct row or column ✔ correct product (d) It is required to multiply all four matrices together so that the final product is a square matrix with the largest possible number of columns. State the order in which the matrices should be multiplied and state the number of columns in the final product. (2 marks) Solution Order: 𝐴× 𝐷× 𝐶× 𝐵. Product will have 3 columns. Specific behaviours ✔ correct order ✔ correct number of columns",
    "questionImage": "apps year 11/generated/resource-previews/docx-pdf-cache-waep-2019-yr11-apps-u1-s1-74f6f0e60d-p008-question-1d9eb035f5.jpg",
