    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN295-173-1 Question 6 (8 marks) Consider the following matrices: 𝐴= [2 0 0 1] 𝐵= [4 5 5 −3] 𝐶= [−2 1 0 2] 𝐷= [−1 1 ] 𝐸= [0 2] 𝐹= [2 −1]. (a) Calculate, where possible, the following. If not possible, give a reason why. (i) 𝐴+ 𝐶. (1 mark) (ii) 𝐷× 𝐵. (1 mark) (iii) 9𝐹−7𝐸. (2 marks) (iv) 𝐵× 𝐶. (2 marks) (b) Calculate 𝐴6. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN295-173-3 Question 6 (8 marks) Consider the following matrices: 𝐴= [2 0 0 1] 𝐵= [4 5 5 −3] 𝐶= [−2 1 0 2] 𝐷= [−1 1 ] 𝐸= [0 2] 𝐹= [2 −1]. (a) Calculate, where possible, the following. If not possible, give a reason why. (i) 𝐴+ 𝐶. (1 mark) (ii) 𝐷× 𝐵. (1 mark) (iii) 9𝐹−7𝐸. (2 marks) (iv) 𝐵× 𝐶. (2 marks) (b) Calculate 𝐴6. (2 marks) Solution [2 0 0 1] + [−2 1 0 2] = [0 1 0 3] Specific behaviours  calculates result Solution 𝐴2 = [2 0 0 1] [2 0 0 1] = [4 0 0 1] 𝐴3 = [4 0 0 1] [2 0 0 1] = [8 0 0 1] Pattern for 𝐴1,1 continues 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, … 𝐴6 = [64 0 0 1] Specific behaviours  calculates 𝐴2  correct 𝐴6 Solution Not possible, as number of columns in 𝐷 (1) is not the same as the number of rows in 𝐵 (2). Specific behaviours  explanation using matrix dimensions Solution 9[2 −1] −7[0 2] = [18 −9] −[0 14] = [18 −23] Specific behaviours  calculates multiples  calculates difference Solution 𝐵× 𝐶= [4 5 5 −3] [−2 1 0 2] = [ −8 14 −10 −1] Specific behaviours  at least two elements correct  correct result",
