    "calculator": "Calculator-free",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 9 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN295-173-1 Question 7 (10 marks) One weekend, a company operated three boats to run harbour trips. On Saturday, boats 𝐷, 𝐸 and 𝐹 carried 48, 65 and 42 adults, and 24, 44 and 25 children, respectively. (a) Represent this information in the 2 × 3 matrix 𝑋, with adults in the top row, and boats in alphabetical order. (1 mark) On Sunday, a total of 120 adults and 87 children took trips. Boats 𝐷 and 𝐸 carried an equal number of children and boat 𝐸 carried 25 adults. Compared to Saturday, boat 𝐹 took 1 more adult and 10 more children. (b) Represent the Sunday passenger numbers in matrix 𝑌, using the same row and column labels as matrix 𝑋. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-FREE 9 APPLICATIONS UNIT 1 See next page SN295-173-3 Question 7 (10 marks) One weekend, a company operated three boats to run harbour trips. On Saturday, boats 𝐷, 𝐸 and 𝐹 carried 48, 65 and 42 adults, and 24, 44 and 25 children, respectively. (a) Represent this information in the 2 × 3 matrix 𝑋, with adults in the top row, and boats in alphabetical order. (1 mark) On Sunday, a total of 120 adults and 87 children took trips. Boats 𝐷 and 𝐸 carried an equal number of children and boat 𝐸 carried 25 adults. Compared to Saturday, boat 𝐹 took 1 more adult and 10 more children. (b) Represent the Sunday passenger numbers in matrix 𝑌, using the same row and column labels as matrix 𝑋. (3 marks) Solution Boat 𝐹: 42 + 1 = 43 adults and 25 + 10 = 35 children. Adults in boat 𝐷: 120 −43 −25 = 52. Split remaining 87 −35 = 52 children, 52 ÷ 2 = 26 in boats 𝐷 and 𝐸. 𝑌= [52 25 43 26 26 35] Specific behaviours  column 𝐷  column 𝐸  column 𝐹 Solution 𝑋= [48 65 42 24 44 25] Specific behaviours  correct matrix",
