    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN104-163-1 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 6 (7 marks) (a) Three straight-lines 𝐿𝐿1, 𝐿𝐿2 and 𝐿𝐿3 are shown on the graph at right. Explain how the graph can be used to solve the simultaneous equations 𝑦𝑦= −2𝑥𝑥+ 10 and 𝑦𝑦= 𝑥𝑥+ 4 and state their solution. (3 marks) (b) A cafe can buy disposable coffee cups from two suppliers, 𝑃𝑃 and 𝑄𝑄. Supplier 𝑃𝑃 charges a delivery fee of $8 plus 3 cents per cup whilst supplier 𝑄𝑄 has no delivery fee but charges 5 cents per cup. Let 𝑦𝑦 be the total cost in cents of buying 𝑥𝑥 cups from a supplier. (i) Write an equation relating 𝑥𝑥 and 𝑦𝑦 for supplier 𝑃𝑃. (1 mark) (ii) Write an equation relating 𝑥𝑥 and 𝑦𝑦 for supplier 𝑄𝑄. (1 mark) (iii) Determine the number of cups for which the total cost is the same for both suppliers and state what this total cost is. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 8 CALCULATOR-FREE See next page SN104-163-3 Question 6 (7 marks) (a) Three straight-lines 𝐿𝐿1, 𝐿𝐿2 and 𝐿𝐿3 are shown on the graph at right. Explain how the graph can be used to solve the simultaneous equations 𝑦𝑦= −2𝑥𝑥+ 10 and 𝑦𝑦= 𝑥𝑥+ 4 and state their solution. (3 marks) (b) A cafe can buy disposable coffee cups from two suppliers, 𝑃𝑃 and 𝑄𝑄. Supplier 𝑃𝑃 charges a delivery fee of $8 plus 3 cents per cup whilst supplier 𝑄𝑄 has no delivery fee but charges 5 cents per cup. Let 𝑦𝑦 be the total cost in cents of buying 𝑥𝑥 cups from a supplier. (i) Write an equation relating 𝑥𝑥 and 𝑦𝑦 for supplier 𝑃𝑃. (1 mark) (ii) Write an equation relating 𝑥𝑥 and 𝑦𝑦 for supplier 𝑄𝑄. (1 mark) (iii) Determine the number of cups for which the total cost is the same for both suppliers and state what this total cost is. (2 marks) Solution 𝑦𝑦= 800 + 3𝑥𝑥 Specific behaviours  correct equation Solution Read from the graph the point of intersection of 𝐿𝐿2 and 𝐿𝐿3. The solution is 𝑥𝑥= 2, 𝑦𝑦= 6. Specific behaviours  states point of intersection of two lines  chooses correct lines  correct solution Solution 800 + 3𝑥𝑥= 5𝑥𝑥 2𝑥𝑥= 800 𝑥𝑥= 400 c 𝑦𝑦= 5 × 400 = $20 Total cost is the same when 40",
