    "calculator": "Calculator-assumed",
    "questionText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN153-183-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 11 (5 marks) A single person living away from home and looking for a job qualifies for a government allowance of $496.50 per fortnight, provided their fortnightly income is no more than $150. In any fortnight that such a person earns more than $150, the allowance will reduce by 50 cents for each dollar of income they have between $150 and $250. If their income is over $250, the payment will further reduce by 60 cents for each dollar of income over $250. Chi qualifies for the allowance. Each week he works two, 5-hour shifts and one, 4-hour shift at a hotel that pays $20.75 per hour. (a) Calculate his fortnightly earnings. (2 marks) (b) Determine his fortnightly government allowance. (3 marks)",
    "answerText": "CALCULATOR-ASSUMED 5 APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 See next page SN153-183-4 Question 11 (5 marks) A single person living away from home and looking for a job qualifies for a government allowance of $496.50 per fortnight, provided their fortnightly income is no more than $150. In any fortnight that such a person earns more than $150, the allowance will reduce by 50 cents for each dollar of income they have between $150 and $250. If their income is over $250, the payment will further reduce by 60 cents for each dollar of income over $250. Chi qualifies for the allowance. Each week he works two, 5-hour shifts and one, 4-hour shift at a hotel that pays $20.75 per hour. (a) Calculate his fortnightly earnings. (2 marks) (b) Determine his fortnightly government allowance. (3 marks) Solution Hours = (2 × 5 + 4) × 2 = 28 Earnings = 28 × 20.75 = $581 Specific behaviours  indicates correct hours worked each fortnight  calculates earnings Solution Reduction 1 = (250 −150) × 0.5 = $50 Reduction 2 = (581 −250) × 0.6 = 331 × 0.6 = $198.60 Allowance = 496.50 −50 −198.60 = $247.90 Specific behaviours  indicates first reduction  indicates second reduction  correct allowance",
