    "questionText": "APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 10 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN153-183-2 DO NOT WRITE IN THIS AREA AS IT WILL BE CUT OFF Question 15 (8 marks) A plastic wedge is shown, not to scale. It takes the form of a prism, with a length of 72 mm and a right triangular cross section measuring 28 mm by 45 mm. (a) Calculate the volume of the plastic wedge. (2 marks) (b) Calculate the total surface area of the plastic wedge. (4 marks) (c) A scale model of the plastic wedge shown has a length of 24 mm. Determine the total surface area of the scale model. (2 marks)",
    "answerText": "APPLICATIONS UNITS 1&2 10 CALCULATOR-ASSUMED See next page SN153-183-4 Question 15 (8 marks) A plastic wedge is shown, not to scale. It takes the form of a prism, with a length of 72 mm and a right triangular cross section measuring 28 mm by 45 mm. (a) Calculate the volume of the plastic wedge. (2 marks) (b) Calculate the total surface area of the plastic wedge. (4 marks) (c) A scale model of the plastic wedge shown has a length of 24 mm. Determine the total surface area of the scale model. (2 marks) Solution Scale factor: 𝑓= 72 ÷ 24 = 3 times smaller TSA of model: 𝐴= 10 322 ÷ 32 = 1148 mm2 Specific behaviours  indicates scale factor  correct TSA Solution 𝐴= 0.5 × 45 × 28 = 630 𝑉= 630 × 72 = 45 360 mm3 Specific behaviours  area of triangle  volume of wedge Solution Let 𝑥 be length of hypotenuse of triangle. 𝑥= √282 + 452 = 53 Area of rectangles: 𝐴= 72 × 53 + 72 × 45 + 72 × 28 = 9072 Area of triangles: 𝐴= 2 × 630 = 1260 TSA: 𝐴= 9072 + 1260 = 10 332 mm2 Specific behaviours  hypotenuse of triangle  area of rectangles  area of triangles  correct TSA",
